// Printability — the second generated table, and it is a *range* table rather than a map.
//
// `packages/unicode/src/printable.wac` is the other file headed *"Generated by tools/gentables.ts.
// Do not edit."*: 733 sorted disjoint ranges as `PRINT_KEYS[i]` (first of a range) and
// `PRINT_VALUES[i]` (last of it), covering 292,464 printable code points.
//
// **Same six words, different query.** `./tables.wac`'s pairs are *(key, value)* and its lookup is
// *find the key*; these are *(first, last)* and the lookup is *find the last range starting at or
// before `cp`, then one comparison against its end*. The array shapes are identical and the searches
// are not — the map's returns on a hit, this one continues to a bounds check — which is why one
// `Entry` type does not serve both and the shipped file writes a second search.
//
// So the generated pair is two shapes wearing one representation. That is worth naming because the
// representation is what a reader sees: two files, four arrays, and nothing distinguishes *this pair
// is a mapping* from *this pair is an interval*.
//
// ## And the file says why it is a separate file, which is a linker fact
//
// > Kept apart from `tables.wac` on purpose: a module's constant arrays are all emitted when it is
// > linked, so sharing a file would make every caller of `isPrintable` carry the case tables too.
//
// **A file boundary standing in for dead-code elimination.** The unit of inclusion is the module, so
// the author split one logical package across two files to keep 65 KB out of programs that only ask
// *is this printable*. Nothing in the language expresses *include this constant only if reached* and
// nothing measures what it costs to get it wrong; the fix is a directory layout and the reason is in
// a comment.

import { Bytes } from "core";

/**
 * One inclusive range of code points.
 *
 * Not `./tables.wac`'s `Entry`, and the difference is the whole of the entry above: `Entry { from;
 * to; }` reads as *from maps to to* and this is *first through last*. Two fields, two `i32`s, one
 * layout, and opposite meanings — which is the `WrongSize { want, got }` against
 * `BadFirstOffset { got, want }` shape at a type rather than at a field order.
 */
export struct Range { i32 first; i32 last; }

/**
 * The printable ranges, sorted and disjoint.
 *
 * *Sorted and disjoint* is what makes the search correct and is a property no signature holds — the
 * fourth thing in this directory that is true of a value and unsayable about its type, after a sorted
 * `Vec` for `uniqueByKey`, a `Table` sorted by key, and a `Bytes` that must be UTF-8.
 */
export struct Ranges {
  Range[] of;

  /** Whether `cp` falls in one. */
  bool has(const this, i32 cp) { … }
}

/** Assigned, and not a control, a surrogate, or a line or paragraph separator. */
export bool isPrintable(i32 cp) { … }

// ## What could not be written
//
// **The two searches differ by one line and neither is shareable.** A map's binary search returns on
// equality; a range table's runs to exhaustion, keeps the last candidate, and then checks its end.
// So `../../../core/order.wac`'s `bytesCmp`-shaped comparator does not help, and neither does a
// generic `binarySearch(xs, key, cmp)` — the second query is *predecessor*, not *find*. A `core`
// that had both would need to name the difference, and this directory has not proposed either.
//
// **And `has` is a `bool`, which is right here and is the reason to say so.** A caller asking *is
// this printable* wants one bit and there is no second question hiding behind it — unlike the 78
// `bool` functions `../../../QUESTIONS.md` counts that collapse a refusal into a yes-or-no. Worth
// recording as the counterexample: the shape is only a smell when the false case has structure, and
// *not printable* has none.
